{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And Moses went up, etc.</b> One needs to know why Moses went up before HaShem called him. Further, one needs to know to what place he went up — if to the mountain, it should have said explicitly 'to the mountain.' And our Sages of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 28) that this is what the verse means when it says (Psalms 68:19) ״עָלִיתָ לַמָּרוֹם״ and so forth. According to their words it is further difficult, for we find that afterward the call to him came from the mountain, as it is written ״וַיִּקְרָא וְגוֹ׳ מִן הָהָר״ — and if he had already ascended to heaven, what room is there for a call from a lower place to a higher place? There is further a fine point to examine: why did the verse say <b>to Elohim</b> and not say 'to HaShem,' as it concludes by saying ״וַיִּקְרָא אֵלָיו ה׳״ and so forth?" "<b>However,</b> the intention of the verse is this: since HaShem had previously said to Moses at the bush (Exodus 3:12) ״בְּהוֹצִיאֲךָ אֶת הָעָם תַּעַבְדוּן אֶת הָאֱלֹהִים עַל הָהָר הַזֶּה״, He was showing him that there is the place of receiving the Torah. Therefore, upon arriving there, Moses acted the part of a faithful servant and anticipated by going up to the mountain. And there was no need to mention 'the mountain,' since it was mentioned just before, as it is written ״נֶגֶד הָהָר״, and it is to that which his saying <b>and Moses went up</b> refers. And his saying <b>to Elohim</b> explains the reason for his ascent — for what was the purpose of this ascent? Therefore it says ״to Elohim,״ because there had preceded, from the Creator, ״תַּעַבְדוּן אֶת הָאֱלֹהִים עַל הָהָר הַזֶּה.״ Therefore, had he waited until HaShem would call him, it would have appeared as laxness and a lack of desire for the matter; therefore he immediately went ahead and prepared himself and ascended. And were it not for what had preceded him in HaShem's utterance ״תַּעַבְדוּן אֶת הָאֱלֹהִים,״ he would not have gone up to the mountain. With this, all the fine points we raised are resolved. And in this interpretation there is no contradiction to the words of our Sages of blessed memory who said (Exodus Rabbah ibid.) ״עָלִיתָ לַמָּרוֹם,״ for in his ascent of the mountain — which is the place where HaShem lowered the heavens upon it — behold, he is found ascending to heaven." "<b>And HaShem called to him.</b> Meaning: when he anticipated and went up, HaShem immediately called him. And you should know that the aspect of holiness does not precede except to one who invites it and arouses toward the matter, and this is their saying of blessed memory (Zohar 3:92a): 'By the arousal below, there is arousal above' (be-it'aruta dil-tata, it'aruta dil-eila), and it is the sod of the verse (Genesis 2:6) ״וְאֵד יַעֲלֶה מִן הָאָרֶץ וְהִשְׁקָה״ — from below upward — ״אֶת כָּל פְּנֵי הָאֲדָמָה״ — from above downward. And <b>וַיִּקְרָא</b> alludes to a language of honor and greatness that HaShem bestowed upon Moses for his readiness and zeal in the matter." "<b>From the mountain, saying.</b> Meaning: because the word of HaShem comes from the heaven of heavens, for HaShem had not yet descended upon Mount Sinai. Therefore the verse comes to inform us that HaShem commanded the voice to pass through the mountain, and from there Moses would hear His words, and it would not turn to the sides, nor would Moses' ear hear it before it reached the mountain — rather, to the mountain it would come, and from the mountain it would be heard by him. And this is the ellipsis of the verse: <b>וַיִּקְרָא אֵלָיו ה׳</b> from the place where His name is, as is known, and this call <b>from the mountain, saying</b> — meaning, from the mountain the speech began, and not before, and not from the sides. And had the verse not said the word ״לֵאמֹר,״ it would have been understood that the beginning of the call was from the mountain and the Shekhinah had already descended there — but such is not the case." "<b>Thus you shall say, etc. You have seen, etc.</b> One needs to know why it doubled <b>you shall say</b> and so forth, <b>and you shall tell</b> and so forth. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Exodus Rabbah 28:2): ״בֵּית יַעֲקֹב״ — these are the women, and to them he should speak a soft reply, and ״לִבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — things as hard as sinews (gidim). And it is difficult: do we not find that HaShem said in His words two types of mission, but rather one expression for all of them together, men and women — if they are harsh, let them hear it together, and if they are soft, let them hear it together? And one cannot say that when Moses speaks he would change the wording from the order spoken, heaven forbid, for you find that at the end of HaShem's words He said to him (Exodus 19:6) ״אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר תְּדַבֵּר אֶל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל,״ and they said of blessed memory (Mekhilta) in this language: 'not less and not more.' And when we say that the intention of <b>to the children of Israel</b> refers to the men, there will be lacking a manner in which he would speak to the women. Moreover, I do not see harsh things in HaShem's words to the men, but rather things that give life to the soul." "<b>Now</b> the intent of the words of HaShem is along this line. First I will preface the concept that holds among us concerning the entire Torah: that the measures of the Master, the Elohim of Israel, are to do good, and His desire to bestow good exceeds even the desire of the recipient to receive it — this is because of the surpassing greatness of the aspect of His goodness, and especially toward the children of Israel. And He, blessed be He, devises plans to multiply our reward and our benefit, for this is what YHWH desires. Now, we find that He revealed His knowledge that the reward of one who fulfills the Torah of YHWH and His commandments out of fear is half the reward of one who does so out of love, from two verses that address the reward for commandments: in one He said (Deuteronomy 7:9) ״לְאֶלֶף דּוֹר״, and in one He said (Exodus 20:6) ״לַאֲלָפִים״. And He was precise with the minister of the thousand — that is the one who observes out of fear — and the minister of the thousands — that is the one who acts out of love." "Now, there is an aspect within the quality of love that causes harm, for its possessor will not take sufficient care to be continually on guard so as never to transgress occasionally by happenstance, for he will say that on account of his closeness to the Creator and His belovedness, He will not be strict with him over this, in the manner and order customary among created beings. And to give you understanding in this matter, you learn it from Moses himself: on account of his great closeness and intimacy with HaShem, he erred and spoke to Him (Exodus 4:13) ״שְׁלַח נָא בְּיַד תִּשְׁלָח״, ״לָמָה הֲרֵעֹתָה״ (Exodus 5:22), ״לֹא הִצַּלְתָּ״, and so forth. And this was caused precisely by the greatness of his closeness before HaShem, which cooled the fear — for it is obvious that another person who would not be so close would have terror and dread fall upon him. Now, the Master, YHWH of Hosts, does not reckon so, as the verse states (Deuteronomy 10:17) ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא יִשָּׂא פָנִים וְלֹא״ and so forth, and on the contrary He is all the more strict with His beloved to discipline him with abundant additional severity for his transgression, even once and even over the lightest of the light, as it says (Psalms 50:3) ״וּסְבִיבָיו נִשְׂעֲרָה מְאֹד״, and they said, of blessed memory (Bava Kamma 50a), that He is exacting with His devout ones as a hair's breadth." "Therefore, at the time of the giving of the Torah, HaShem devised good thoughts to benefit them through the acceptance of the Torah, and before Him were two paths: the first — to speak words of love and affection, and there is in this an ultimate good when they accept it out of love, for the sacred weight is doubled in their reward. But there is another aspect to this as well: one who acts out of love sometimes does not attend to a single detail of the Torah, like the way of a father with his craving and dandling son — since there is upon him no aspect of fear — whereas when the acceptance of the Torah is out of fear, the way of the one who fears is to tremble over a single detail like a slave before his master, and thus he is found standing constantly to guard the tree of life; and so it is in their words, of blessed memory (Yerushalmi Berakhot). And the second path is to speak hard words, like a king who decrees upon his servant with an injection of bitterness and the awe of sovereignty, and there is in this a good end: that they will not treat any of the commandments of the Torah lightly, not even occasionally and by chance — except that they receive half the reward compared to the reward that reaches them when they act out of love." "<b>Therefore</b> HaShem devised to command in two modes — in the way of love and in the way of sovereignty and intimidation — and He expressed both of them with the sweet tongue of a righteous one in a single bearing. And this is what He began to say: <b>כֹּה תֹאמַר</b>, along this line: ״כֹּה״ — meaning, in this order that I am saying to you, <b>תֹאמַר לְבֵית יַעֲקֹב וְתַגֵּיד</b> and so forth — meaning, there is in it a soft utterance, which is the way of love and affection, <b>וְתַגֵּיד</b> — meaning, there are in it also hard things like sinews (גִידִים), which is the way of intimidation and fear. And He was precise to say <b>בֵּית יַעֲקֹב</b> with the soft utterance, and <b>וּבְנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל</b> with the hard one, to indicate the portion lacking for each of the two along this line: for the house of Jacob are the lesser rank of our holy nation, who will not attain service of HaShem out of love but only out of fear — therefore the commandment applies to them that they must add service out of love, alluded to in the soft utterance as mentioned. And to the children of Israel, who are the chosen and exalted rank that attains the aspect of love, He said in their regard <b>וְתַגֵּיד</b> — that the aspect of love alone does not suffice for them regarding even a single detail, and they need both fear and love, for the reason we have written. You thus find that both are good and both are needed for the fulfillment of the Torah, and every person of Israel must acquire both of them — love and fear. And both of them together were spoken by El Elyon in the pleasantness of His words when He said ״אַתֶּם רְאִיתֶם אֲשֶׁר עָשִׂיתִי״ and so forth, as I will explain in the forthcoming passage with the help of HaShem. And the words of our Sages, of blessed memory, who said (Mekhilta) — these are the women and these are the men — that is the way of midrash."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:30:36.425169876Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}